{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "383",
    "case_summary": "An adolescent girl presented with an asymptomatic slightly reddish verrucous plaque on the right temple. The skin lesion was nail sized when first noticed 1½ years earlier, then gradually developed over the 8 months prior to presentation. The patient denied any associated fevers, weight loss, or night sweats.\n\nPhysical examination revealed a slightly reddish verrucous mass with translucent papules and plaques on the right temple, about 5 cm in diameter, with surface hemorrhagic crust in the side adjacent to right eyebrow arch. The lesion was nontender with no purulent discharge and no severe necrotic changes. There was no hepatosplenomegaly or superficial lymphadenopathy.\n\n- Laboratory tests: Blood cell counts, urinalysis, and kidney and hepatic panels revealed no abnormalities.- Imaging studies:  - Cerebral CT and MRI findings: Skin thickening and a soft-tissue mass in the right frontotemporal region near the orbit  - Chest CT and abdominal ultrasonography: No evidence of metastases- Histopathology:  Image Title: Figure B  Image Description: Papillomatous epidermal hyperplasia, edema of dermal papillae, and neoplastic infiltration consisting of small round blue cells with alternating areas of dense and loose cellularity  Image Title: Figure C  Image Description: Small, round blue cells in the dermis with round, fusiform, or oval hyperchromatic nuclei, conspicuous nucleoli, and scant cytoplasm  Image Title: Figure D  Image Description: Tumor cells positive for MyoD1- Immunohistochemistry: Positive for MyoD1, myogenin, vimentin, and desmin. Negative for myeloperoxidase, CD45, CK, Ki-67, CD31, CD34, CD1a, CD117, and SOX-10.",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Progressive Unilateral Facial Mass with Verrucous Appearance and Neoplastic Infiltration",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.8,
